            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "friendly ping for review, do we want to consider this patch or should i abandon it instead.."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Brad Bishop",
                        "email": "bradleyb@fuzziesquirrel.com",
                        "username": "bradbishop"
                    },
                    "message": "I appreciate the effort here to improve the code coverage.  I support using constexpr over cpp macros.\n\nAnother approach that might not be as abstract and avoid the extra reference would be to just enable MAPPER_ENABLE_DEBUG by default for any build, not just debug builds (and disabling it by default in the recipe).\n\nAnother thought is changing the name from DEBUG to PROFILE since that is what is actually going on here, and removing the linkage to debug builds."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "> would be to just enable MAPPER_ENABLE_DEBUG by default for any build, \nnot just debug builds (and disabling it by default in the recipe).\n\nThis will not improve coverage of the production code path. We would be running static analysis on a debug build and then compiling something else for the full image, defeating the point of doing static analysis in the first place.\n\n> Another thought is changing the name from DEBUG to PROFILE since that is what is actually going on here, and removing the linkage \nto debug builds.\n\nMade a new revision which has the class members for profiling present by default but still guards the code using those members.\n\nI believe this will introduce only minimal overhead (allocating a few extra bytes for storing the extra class members) while keeping the implementation simple without templated base class.\n\nLet me know if this new patchset works better \ud83d\udc4d"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Brad Bishop",
                        "email": "bradleyb@fuzziesquirrel.com",
                        "username": "bradbishop"
                    },
                    "message": "> This will not improve coverage of the production code path.\n\nIt doesn't hurt it though either does it?\n\n> We would be running static analysis on a debug build and then compiling something else for the full image, defeating the point of doing static analysis in the first place.\n\nWe enable meson options by default for static analysis and CI purposes and then turn them off by default in the recipe in many places in OpenBMC.  I'm not sure why this is any different.\n\n> Let me know if this new patchset works better \ud83d\udc4d\n\nIt does, TY!"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "> We enable meson options by default for static analysis and CI purposes and then turn them off by default in the recipe in many places in OpenBMC. I'm not sure why this is any different.\n\nIt makes sense to enable feature options for CI to get the best coverage for build testing and tidy.\n\nWith debug options i am not sure if it should be enabled in CI, it can depend on the option and whats behind it. Overall the code section which is enabled will get the better coverage from tidy.\n\nIn below example there is a debug code path which counts the number of calls. The production code path has a bug and would not compile but the compiler cannot see that because its a template.\n\n```\n\n#include <type_traits>\n\n#define DEBUG 1\ntemplate <typename T>\nvoid f(T value)\n{\n\tif constexpr (DEBUG)\n\t{\n\t\tvalue += 1;\n\t}\n\telse\n\t{\n\t\tvalue.this_does_not_exist();\n\t}\n}\n\nint main(){\n\n\tf(3);\n\treturn 0;\n}\n```\n\nIn that example it would be best to build the production code path in CI, and the bug would be found.\n\nFor this patch, i would have to agree with you, the debug code path is mostly additional code and we would probably not lose much coverage of the production code path. But for me its not a clear choice but a tradeoff which depends on code details which may change over time.\n\nWorst case if we build production code path in CI is that the debug code path gets bricked. Worst case of building the debug code path in CI is that tidy misses a bug in the production code path."
                }
            ],
